    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Pacific halibut, coastwide (Canada and US)\"\nparams:\n  stock_id: \"halibut\"\n---\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n:::\n\n\n::: {.disclaimer}\nExploratory personal project. Not an official assessment and not a seafood recommendation.\n:::\n\n::::: {.columns}\n\n:::: {.column width=\"36%\"}\n\n::: {.cell}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"stock-card\">\n<div class=\"card-head\" style=\"background:#16a085;\">\n<div class=\"card-species\">Pacific halibut</div>\n<div class=\"card-title\">Pacific halibut, coastwide (Canada and US)</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"badges\">\n<span class=\"badge\" style=\"background:#1a7f4b;\">DFO 2024: Healthy</span>\n<span class=\"badge tier\">Data tier A</span>\n</div>\n<div class=\"stat\">1.88× LRP</div>\n<div class=\"stat-label\">Biomass relative to the limit reference point, 2020</div>\n<img class=\"spark\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Sparkline of biomass relative to the limit reference point for Pacific halibut, coastwide (Canada and US)\"/>\n<dl>\n<dt>Evidence</dt>\n<dd>Biomass and fishing pressure relative to MSY</dd>\n<dt>Managed by</dt>\n<dd>DFO, co-managed with United States of America via the International Pacific Halibut Commission</dd>\n<dt>Status method</dt>\n<dd>Scientific Peer Review Process</dd>\n<dt>RAM assessment period</dt>\n<dd>1992-2020</dd>\n<dt>Biomass estimates</dt>\n<dd>1992 to 2020</dd>\n</dl>\n<div class=\"fun\">Fun fact: The largest flatfish in the North Pacific. As it grows, both eyes end up on one side of its head.</div>\n<div class=\"note\">Coastwide stock; pin position is illustrative.</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n```\n\n:::\n:::\n\n::::\n\n:::: {.column width=\"4%\"}\n::::\n\n:::: {.column width=\"60%\"}\n\n::: {.cell}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n![](halibut_files/figure-html/trend-1.png){width=768}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\n[Download the 2-page rapid assessment (PDF)](../reports/output/rapid_assessment_halibut.pdf){.btn .btn-primary}\n::::\n\n:::::\n\n\n## Kobe plot\n\n![](halibut_files/figure-html/kobe-1.png){width=75%}\n\n## Management context\n\n\nText in quotation marks is copied exactly as published in DFO's 2024 Sustainability Survey for Fisheries, including any typos.\n\n- **Limit reference point, as described by DFO:** “LRP set at 0.2 of the equilibrium un-fished female spawning biomass (SB20). At the end of 2023, SB was estimated to be 174,000,000 net lbs. From this, SB20 = 34,800,000 net lbs.”\n- **Upper stock reference, as described by DFO:** “USR set at 0.3 of the equilibrium un-fished female spawning biomass (SB30). At the end of 2023, SB was estimated to be 174,000,000 net lbs. From this, SB30 = 52,200,000 net lbs.”\n- **Limit used for this project's indicator:** SSBlim from RAM (IPHC limit; the 2024 survey describes the LRP as 0.2 x unfished female spawning biomass)\n- **Management plan, as reported by DFO:** “Pacific Integrated Fisheries Management Plan - GROUNDFISH - Effective Feb 21, 2024 - Feb 20, 2025 (Current Pacific Integrated Fisheries Management plan - GROUNDFISH - Effective Feb 21, 2025 - Feb 20, 2026)”\n- **Managed by:** DFO, co-managed with United States of America via the International Pacific Halibut Commission\n\n\n**Science documents cited by DFO in the 2024 survey:**\n\n- <https://www.iphc.int/uploads/2023/12/IPHC-2024-AM100-10-Data-overview-and-stock-assessment.pdf>\n- <https://www.iphc.int/uploads/2023/12/IPHC-2024-AM100-12-Projections-and-harvest-decision-table.pdf>\n\n## Data gaps and caveats\n\n\n- Time series ends in 2020; the DFO zone is from the 2024 survey.\n- Fishing-pressure ratio available only to 2013.\n- Limit reference point taken or derived from RAM Legacy; DFO's current value may differ.\n\n[← Back to all stock cards](../cards.qmd)\n\n",
